7592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb09ac10-eb5c-5627-9b3a-f58793f9c5b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-80089" :question/problem "Let $n\\in Z.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\,$ If $______,$ then $n>0.$\n\nWhich of the following could be placed into the blank space above to create a statement whose proof is vacuous?\n\n- $n<0$\n- $0>1$\n- ${n}^{2}<0$" :question/worked-solution "We say that an implication has a *vacuous proof* if the antecedent is always false: \n\n$$\n\\overset{\\text{Always false}}{\\overset{⏞}{P}}⇒\\,Q\n$$\n\nIt does not matter whether the consequent is true or false. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our antecedents in turn.\n\n- Predicate I, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is *not* always false. $\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement II is always false. $\\,✓$\n\n- Predicate III, defined for $n\\in Z,$ is always false. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f645c498-658b-5078-9fb6-4e4c5b6c865d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155686} {:answer/id #uuid "1d30b730-6613-56c6-96cb-30e7c9cf7bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155687} {:answer/id #uuid "37edff3d-d6a2-5dac-b9ac-c83470e59018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155688} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1a4a6e-cc6e-544f-9226-76312cefbec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 1759